# fork.patch is a GENERATED `git diff` of the CloudRetro fork, and its CONTENT legitimately contains
# CR bytes (the fork's own sources are CRLF). With core.autocrlf=true and no rule here, git rewrote
# every line to CRLF on checkout — 72,004 CRs became 91,664, the file grew 19,660 bytes, and
# `git apply` then rejected the patch outright. The rebuild path was therefore broken from any fresh
# clone, while export-arcade-fork.ps1 still reported "the backup is real": it verifies the file it
# has just written to disk, never the one git hands back after a round trip.
#
# -text = never convert line endings on checkin or checkout. The bytes committed are the bytes
# restored, which is the only thing that makes this patch a usable backup.
docker/arcade/patches/*.patch -text
# Same reasoning, same hazard, different directory: flycast-custom-core.patch is the rebuild path for
# flycast_custom_libretro.dll and had NO rule until 2026-08-08 — it sat one `git checkout` away from
# the identical breakage. Any generated patch under docker/arcade belongs here.
docker/arcade/*.patch -text
# The two rules above are ANCHORED (a pattern with a slash is matched from this directory and * never
# crosses one), so neither of them reached docker/arcade/patches/gst/ - the nvcodec patches that ARE
# the rebuild path for libgstnvcodec.dll, i.e. for temporal SVC, intra-refresh and (2026-08-08) the
# reference discipline. They sat one fresh clone away from the exact fork.patch breakage described
# above. ** crosses directories, so this catches any generated patch under docker/arcade, wherever
# someone files the next one.
docker/arcade/**/*.patch -text
